-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Precision:** Ray re-hitting the same face due to floating-point error is handled by advancing the ray origin by $10^{-4}$ after each intersection or sorting and filtering hit distances [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Open Meshes:** Single-direction ray casting yields incorrect results for non-watertight meshes. Use multiple random directions and voting [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Double-thick Meshes:** Standard odd-even rule fails for double-sided surfaces. Use $(N_{hits}/2) \% 2 == 1$ [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Malformed Input:** OBJ/STL files with non-multiple-of-3 position/index arrays must be validated and rejected [S4, unofficial].
+### Parameters
+*   `resolution`: Voxel grid spacing (float). Must be $> 10^{-6}$ to prevent division by zero or excessive memory [S4, unofficial]. Default varies (e.g., 0.5 in `voxelizer-rs`, 3.0 in GUI) [S3, S4, unofficial].
+*   `samples`: Number of sample rays per voxel (int). Default 1 (single ray). Higher values handle open meshes [S2, unofficial].
+*   `fill_interior`: Boolean. If true, fills enclosed empty voxels. Default True [S3, unofficial].
+*   `narrow_band`: Float. Distance threshold for SDF. Expands bounds and filters voxels. Default None [S4, unofficial].
+*   `surface_only`: Boolean. If true, retains only voxels intersecting the surface, ignoring interior. Default False [S4, unofficial].
+*   `double_thick`: Boolean. Handles double-sided geometry by checking `(intersections/2)%2 == 1`. Default False [S2, unofficial].
+
+### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
+*   **Ray Re-hitting**: Floating-point precision can cause a ray to intersect the same face multiple times. Mitigated by advancing the ray origin by a small epsilon ($10^{-4}$) after each hit [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Open Meshes**: Single-ray casting fails for non-watertight meshes. Mitigated by multi-directional sampling and voting [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Double-Thick Meshes**: Standard odd-even rule fails. Requires specific parity check `(intersections/2)%2 == 1` [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Precision Ordering**: Intersection points may be returned out of order due to precision errors. Sorting is required if exact distance ordering is needed, though simple counting often suffices for parity [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Usage**: High resolutions generate massive files. A 256x256x256 resolution can produce >4.7 GB OBJ files [S2, unofficial]. Use binary formats (binvox) for high res.
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Solid Voxelization based on Octree](2062-solid-voxelization-based-on-octree.md)
-Solid Voxelization based on Octree is a method for converting triangular surface meshes into a solid voxel representation using an adaptive octree structure. It determines the solid or empty status of leaf nodes by combining ray casting for sign determination with closest-point distance calculations. Unlike fixed-grid voxelization, this method adapts resolution to the geometry, potentially reducing node count for sparse scenes, while its reliance on ray casting for sign determination distinguishes it from exact geometric intersection methods like [Octree-based 3D Voxelization with SAT Pruning](2588-octree-based-3d-voxelization-with-sat-pruning.md).
+Solid Voxelization based on Octree converts a polygon mesh (specifically OBJ format) into a hierarchical octree representation to determine the solid/empty status and distance to surface for each leaf node. It differs from fixed-grid voxelization by using an octree structure for adaptive resolution and from ray-casting-only methods by combining spatial partitioning with ray casting for sign determination. It builds on [Mesh Voxelization](6514-mesh-voxelization.md) by providing the specific octree-based implementation for mesh inputs.
 
 ### Solid Voxelization via 3D Scan-Fill
-An extension of surface voxelization for closed objects that fills the inner region of the object using a 3D scan-filling operation [S5, unofficial]. It traverses the volume slice by slice and line by line, maintaining an inside/outside flag that toggles when the scan line spans a voxel intersecting the object boundary [S5, unofficial]. To eliminate errors from singular points, scan-filling is performed along all three axis directions, and the common voxels across all three results are retained [S5, unofficial].
+An extension of surface voxelization for closed objects that fills the inner region of the object using a 3D scan-filling operation [S5, unofficial]. It traverses the volume slice by slice and line by line, maintaining an inside/outside flag for each scan line that toggles when crossing the object boundary [S5, unofficial]. To eliminate errors from singular points, it performs scan-filling along all three axis directions and intersects the results [S5, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N^3 \cdot T)$, where $N$ is the grid resolution per axis and $T$ is the number of triangles [G]. Each voxel center requires a ray-triangle intersection test against all triangles (or a subset via acceleration structures) [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources [G].
-*   **Performance:** On an NVIDIA GTX 970, a 64x64x64 voxelization of a 69,664 triangle bunny mesh takes 0.88 seconds with 1 sample, scaling to 51.96 seconds at 256x256x256. A 512x512x512 resolution for the same mesh takes 417.62 seconds [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Topologically robust for closed meshes; provides accurate inside/outside determination; supports SDF generation [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Computationally expensive for high resolutions and complex meshes ($O(T)$ per voxel); sensitive to open meshes without multiple sampling; generates large files (e.g., 4.7 GB for 256^3 resolution in OBJ format) [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When topological correctness (watertightness) is critical and the mesh is closed [G].
-*   **When not to use:** For very high-resolution requirements on complex meshes where rasterization-based methods are faster; for open surfaces where scan-conversion is more appropriate [G].
+*   **Time Complexity**: $O(N \cdot M)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels and $M$ is the number of triangles, assuming a naive triangle list. With spatial acceleration structures (BVH, Grid), this reduces to $O(N \cdot \log M)$ or $O(N)$ for uniform grids.
+*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(N)$ for the voxel grid. High resolutions can exceed available RAM (e.g., 4.7 GB for 256^3 resolution) [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance**: GPU acceleration (CUDA) provides massive speedups (400-2750x) over CPU. On an NVIDIA GTX 970, a 512^3 voxelization of a teapot (2464 triangles) took 15.64 seconds [S2, unofficial]. A 1024^3 sphere (960 triangles) took 49.93 seconds [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths**: Robust handling of complex geometry; produces watertight volumes; supports SDF generation.
+*   **Limitations**: Computationally expensive for high resolutions without acceleration; sensitive to mesh quality (non-manifold edges, double-sided faces); large output file sizes for dense grids.
+*   **When to Use**: When watertight, high-quality voxel volumes are required for rendering or simulation, and mesh quality is good.
+*   **When Not to Use**: For real-time applications with very high resolution requirements without GPU support; for meshes with severe topological errors that cannot be fixed.
 
 ## Background
-Mesh voxelization via ray casting is a fundamental technique in computational geometry and computer graphics, widely used in additive manufacturing (3D printing) preparation, medical imaging, and collision detection [G]. The specific CUDA-based implementation detailed in the sources was developed for MIT's 6.807 Computational Fabrication course, leveraging the Möller–Trumbore intersection algorithm for GPU acceleration [S2, unofficial]. The method has been extended to support Signed Distance Fields (SDF) and narrow-band representations for smooth surface reconstruction [S4, unofficial].
+Mesh voxelization via ray casting is a fundamental technique in computational geometry and computer graphics. It is widely used in 3D printing (slicing), volume rendering (marching cubes on voxelized data), and collision detection. The method relies on the ray casting algorithm for point-in-polygon/polyhedron testing, a classic problem in computational geometry. Implementations often leverage GPU parallelism (CUDA, OpenCL) to handle the massive number of ray-triangle intersection tests required for high-resolution grids [S2, unofficial]. The Möller–Trumbore algorithm is the standard efficient method for ray-triangle intersection in this context [S2, unofficial].
 
